hello I'm going to show some interesting  new ways you can create web interfaces  with version 11 in version 10 we  introduced the Wolfram cloud a way to  develop and deploy programs with the  Wolfram language it's true you can write  every part of a web interface yourself  from the ground up and we've made some  of that easier in version 11 with things  like generate HTTP response and URL  dispatcher but in version 11 we've also  added more high-level declarative ways  to create interfaces form page he is  similar to form function but instead of  just showing the results keeps the form  so you can keep interacting with it  let's try it so here we see the form and  let's try three sides so now we see both  the result in the form with form  function you just get this triangle here  with form page we have the form itself  so we can view the result at the same  time as we're entering more input  without having to hit the browser back  button so you can appreciate certain  types of forms will lend themselves to  this interface and certain to form  function form functions good for  something like an online poll you want  to accept inputs show a result and move  on forum page is good for letting a user  kind of explore what changes in response  to different inputs now along with this  is the auto submitting wrapper this is  useful if you want the form to update  whenever a field has changed so this is  similar to the previous form but we've  got this Auto submitting wrapper around  the interpreter specification in this  case we've made it a slider that's  restricted to a certain range of number  of sides let's see how this works  here we've got our slider pick a number  of sides and we can change it now you  may be saying to yourself big deal form  that draws a polygon but that that's not  really the point is it the point is how  little effort it took to produce this  interface with all the HTML CSS and  JavaScript it gets generated for you the  point is to take your ideas and get them  onto the web into the hands of your  users just as quickly let's look at  something a bit different this is a  simple idea and something you definitely  could do yourself but it can be handy  that it's already done for you this  gallery view symbol creates a browsable  gallery of images first note that this  is a simple cloud deploy of a gallery  view expression and there's a list of  expressions here you can just have a  list like this or you can have a title  and labels for everything in an  association so let's look at the version  that already has the the title and  labels so there's a couple of things  that are done automatically for you one  is it handles a layout it's items onto a  row depending on the page size and two  handles pagination in this case there's  so many polyhedra that we've got it  looks like ten pages and it's it's  generated this pagination interface  itself and its handling all that that's  pretty nice set of features to be  handled automatically  next thing we'll look at is ask function  I think this is an incredibly neat idea  the way you use this is you write code  like you would perform page or form  function but you sprinkle the code with  a scrappers these go off to the user and  collect information it works really well  with complicated problems where  sometimes you need information only in  certain cases and based on previous  answers and what better place to go for  an example of something complicated than  the US tax system so this example plays  accountant and attempts to tell you your  marginal tax rate based on asking  questions like your filing status  independence and so on so you can see in  the code it starts by asking about  filing status and gives up gives a form  function like input specification and it  continues with the calculation and let's  see here is a yes or no question do you  have any dependents and only if it gives  a yes answer and it's asking how many so  let's try this  okay so we're going to select our status  give an income level say yes we have  dependents and they come Cheaper by the  Dozen so we're going to say 12 and it  computes our answer that is a pretty  neat little web tool to come from this  small an amount of code let's look at  one last thing so sometimes you don't  know how many fields you're going to  need for a form the repeating element  wrapper lets you have more than one of  the same sort of field in this example  we want to collect multiple addresses so  we can create a map of travel directions  so in this case we specify we want  between two and five street addresses  this is an interpreter type using this  repeating element wrapper and what comes  out when we generate the form is it  automatically shows the the minimum of  two addresses then it has these plus and  minus buttons that let the user add more  or subtract one from its list of  addresses and finally when you submit  the form you see the the map of the trip  again all of this code you need to  handle these various numbers of inputs  is handled for you and all of these  tools and more are available in version  11 and let you get your applications  online hosted in the Wolfram cloud with  relatively very little code thank you
 

